    "case_id": "54",
    "case_summary": "A three-year-old boy, of Nigerian descent, with Sickle Cell Anemia (Hb SS), HbF levels between 8–14%, and a steady state Hb of 6–7 g/dl, diagnosed at birth. At three months old, Hb electrophoresis showed bands predominantly at S and F, with absent A. At six weeks old, he was hospitalized for severe anemia of unknown cause and received his first blood transfusion. During a routine follow-up at age three, he was found to have significant lymph node enlargement. Enlarged nodes were observed in the deep cervical, posterior auricular, and axillary chains, with the largest measuring 2 cm by 2 cm. Nodes were also present in both groins, measuring about 1.5 cm, and were mobile and soft. There was no evidence of ongoing systemic illness, and the examination was otherwise unremarkable, with no hepatosplenomegaly. An ultrasound confirmed the presence of lymph nodes at the hilum of the spleen and liver. The nodes appeared to reduce in size over time. Later that year, he was admitted with an acute vaso-occlusive crisis, and the lymph node enlargement was again noted. Further radiological investigation confirmed multiple enlarged lymph nodes throughout the abdomen. Additional tests, including a complete blood count, blood film, chest radiograph, virology screen, lymphocyte subsets, Heaf test, and HIV test, were all normal. The lymphadenopathy began to resolve spontaneously. At age five, he experienced another recurrence of lymphadenopathy. Examination revealed multiple enlarged lymph nodes in the axillary, cervical, and inguinal regions. The lymph nodes were attached to each other, with a 'grape-like' appearance and 'sponge-like' consistency on palpation.\n\nThere was hyperplasia of the paracortex with abundance of plasma cells. In addition, there were very large epitheliod histiocytes, which contained lymphoid cells within their cytoplasm. The histiocytes stained with S100 protein.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
